Create a Standard Operating Procedure Document
Learning Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Plan the scope of a repeatable business process for documentation
Build a structured SOP template using Markdown
Draft numbered procedure steps with decision points
Create a RACI responsibility matrix
Review an SOP against a quality checklist
Convert an SOP into PDF and HTML formats using open-source tools
Prerequisites
Basic familiarity with a Linux terminal (opening a terminal, typing commands)
No prior technical writing experience required
A text editor (we will use nano, pre-installed on most Linux systems)
Environment Setup
Al Nafi provides a single Linux machine via Start Lab. All work happens on this machine — no external cloud accounts needed.

Start your lab machine and open a terminal.
Update packages and install Pandoc (used to convert Markdown to PDF/HTML):
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y pandoc texlive-latex-base texlive-fonts-recommended
Create a working directory:
mkdir -p ~/sop-lab
cd ~/sop-lab
Tasks
Task 1: Identify the Business Process and Scope
Choose a simple repeatable process. We will use Employee Onboarding as the example.

Create a scope notes file:

nano scope-notes.txt
Add these lines (edit as needed):

Process Name: Employee Onboarding
Scope: Covers new hire's first week, from offer acceptance to system access setup.
Out of Scope: Long-term performance reviews.
Save with Ctrl+O, Enter, then exit with Ctrl+X.

Task 2: Create the SOP Template
Create the main SOP file in Markdown:

nano sop-onboarding.md
Copy this starter template and fill in the blanks (marked with TODO):

# Standard Operating Procedure: Employee Onboarding

## 1. Purpose
<!-- TODO: Explain WHY this SOP exists in 1-2 sentences -->

## 2. Scope
<!-- TODO: Paste scope info from scope-notes.txt -->

## 3. Responsibilities
<!-- TODO: List roles involved, e.g. HR Manager, IT Admin, Direct Manager -->

## 4. Procedure Steps
<!-- Steps go in Task 3 -->

## 5. References
<!-- TODO: List related documents, e.g. "Employee Handbook v2.1" -->

## 6. Revision History

| Version | Date       | Author | Changes         |
|---------|------------|--------|-----------------|
| 0.1     | 2024-01-01 | Your Name | Initial draft |
Save and exit.

Task 3: Draft Procedure Steps with Decision Points
Open the file again and replace the ## 4. Procedure Steps section with numbered steps. Use this pattern for decision points:

## 4. Procedure Steps

1. HR sends offer letter to candidate.
2. Candidate accepts offer.
   - If candidate declines, notify hiring manager and close the process.
3. HR creates employee record in HR system.
4. IT Admin creates system accounts (email, VPN).
   - If access request fails, escalate to IT Support Lead.
5. Direct Manager schedules first-day orientation.
6. New hire completes mandatory compliance training.
7. HR confirms onboarding completion checklist is signed off.
This shows a basic decision branch pattern: main step, then an indented - If... then... line for conditional handling.

Task 4: Add a RACI Matrix
Insert a new section before "References" called "Roles and Responsibilities Matrix":

## 4a. Roles and Responsibilities (RACI) Matrix

| Task                        | HR | IT Admin | Manager | New Hire |
|-----------------------------|----|----------|---------|----------|
| Send offer letter           | R  | -        | A       | I        |
| Create system accounts      | I  | R        | -       | I        |
| Schedule orientation        | I  | -        | R       | I        |
| Complete compliance training| I  | -        | I       | R        |

R = Responsible, A = Accountable, C = Consulted, I = Informed
Save the file.

Task 5: Peer Review Against a Quality Checklist
Create a checklist file and review your SOP manually:

nano checklist.md
Paste the checklist:

# SOP Quality Checklist

- [ ] Purpose is clear and one-line specific
- [ ] Scope defines what is included and excluded
- [ ] All roles in Procedure Steps appear in Responsibilities section
- [ ] Steps are numbered sequentially
- [ ] At least one decision point (if/then) is documented
- [ ] RACI matrix covers all major steps
- [ ] Revision History has at least one entry
Go through your sop-onboarding.md file and manually mark each box [x] if satisfied. Fix any gaps in sop-onboarding.md before continuing.

Task 6: Publish as PDF and HTML
Convert your finished Markdown SOP into both formats using Pandoc:

# Convert to HTML
pandoc sop-onboarding.md -o sop-onboarding.html

# Convert to PDF
pandoc sop-onboarding.md -o sop-onboarding.pdf
List the generated files to confirm:

ls -l sop-onboarding.*
You should see sop-onboarding.md, sop-onboarding.html, and sop-onboarding.pdf.

Verification
Run these checks to confirm successful completion:

Confirm all files exist:
ls ~/sop-lab
Expected output includes: scope-notes.txt, sop-onboarding.md, checklist.md, sop-onboarding.html, sop-onboarding.pdf

View the HTML file content in the terminal:
cat sop-onboarding.html | head -20
You should see HTML tags like <h1>, <table>.

Check the PDF was created with content (non-zero size):
ls -lh sop-onboarding.pdf
File size should be greater than 0 KB (typically 20-100 KB).

Open the HTML file in a browser (if GUI available):
xdg-open sop-onboarding.html
Troubleshooting Tips
Pandoc PDF error about LaTeX: Re-run sudo apt install -y texlive-latex-base and try again.
"command not found: pandoc": Confirm installation completed with pandoc --version.
Table not rendering in PDF: Ensure there are no extra spaces before | characters in Markdown tables.
nano not saving: Make sure to press Ctrl+O then Enter, then Ctrl+X to exit.
File not found errors: Confirm you are in the ~/sop-lab directory using pwd.
Conclusion
In this lab, you completed the full SOP documentation lifecycle on a single Linux machine. You identified a business process (Employee Onboarding), scoped it, and built a structured SOP using a standard template with Purpose, Scope, Responsibilities, Procedure Steps, References, and Revision History sections. You drafted numbered steps with decision points, created a RACI matrix to clarify ownership, reviewed your document against a quality checklist, and published the final SOP as both PDF and HTML using Pandoc. These skills directly support the Documentation Specialist and Business Analyst roles and align with CPTC Foundation-level competencies in structured technical documentation.
